  "narrative": "Final synthesis: no additional response deadlines are listed and the voluntary validation budget is exhausted. I reassessed H203 using its discovery evidence R208 and validation evidence R216. R208 was directionally supportive but did not clear the pre-specified signed +0.10 meaningfulness benchmark in the supplied dataset, with signed estimate +0.186 and 95% interval +0.039 to +0.333. Independent validation R216 gave a similar point estimate, signed estimate +0.206, but the independent interval crossed both null and the +0.10 benchmark, +0.206 with interval -0.055 to +0.467, and it was based on only 30 exposed cells in the narrow stratum. I therefore keep H203 unresolved and closed. This is a precision problem in a small highly restricted contrast, not evidence against the broader accepted PTEN-loss and dependency_STAT3 association, which was independently validated in much larger comparisons. The overall report is that the supplied 2000-sample lung-cancer CRISPR dependency dataset supports five large within-dataset associations under the signed +0.10 practical-significance benchmark, while most other predictor-dependency links and interactions are null, small, inconsistent, or too imprecise. First, MSI-high status is strongly associated with more negative dependency_WRN. Discovery R6 gave +0.790 with interval +0.760 to +0.819, and validation R52 gave +0.802 with interval +0.753 to +0.851. The effect is far above the +0.10 threshold and remained supported in multiple refinement contexts, including TP53-wild-type, Avana library, adenocarcinoma, batch A, high-quality RNA/DNA/CRISPR QC, different mutation-burden bands, and several co-mutation-restricted strata. The magnitude and robustness are striking, but this remains an association in this dataset, not proof that MSI status mechanistically causes WRN dependency. Second, SMARCA4 loss is strongly associated with more negative dependency_SMARCA2. Discovery R8 gave +0.784 with interval +0.765 to +0.803, and validation R13 gave +0.793 with interval +0.763 to +0.822. Subgroup refinements across batch A, Avana, KY, adenocarcinoma, QC-restricted, PIK3CA strata, TP53, STK11, CDKN2A, KEAP1, ARID1A, MYC, and multi-negative co-mutation contexts were consistently supportive. By contrast, ARID1A loss did not show a meaningful SMARCA2 association within SMARCA4-wild-type cells, and the ARID1A interaction was null, supporting specificity for SMARCA4 rather than a general chromatin-remodeling loss effect. Third, KEAP1 loss is associated with much weaker dependency_NFE2L2, i.e. less negative dependency scores. H20 accepted this signed positive direction using discovery R20 +0.796 with interval +0.776 to +0.816 and validation R39 +0.806 with interval +0.775 to +0.838. The opposite H9 direction was rejected. This finding is internally consistent across assay, lineage, mutation-burden, co-mutation, and library subsets, although the broader biological interpretation of KEAP1 loss and NFE2L2 dependency remains uncertain. Fourth, TP53 loss is associated with weaker dependency_USP7. H15 accepted discovery R17 +0.396 with interval +0.325 to +0.467, and validation R26 was nearly identical, +0.395 with interval +0.293 to +0.498. Multiple refinement strata across library, TP53-cooccurring genotypes, QC, lineage, mutation burden, STK11, CDKN2A, ARID1A, KEAP1, PIK3CA, MYC, MTAP, and co-mutation-negative backgrounds were supportive. The only notable caution is the mutation-burden interaction: the discovery interaction H148 did not provide robust validation, R153 was negative and crossed the benchmark, so I do not support a meaningful TP53-loss by high mutation-burden modification of the USP7 association. Fifth, PTEN loss is associated with more negative dependency_STAT3. H93 accepted discovery R88 +0.267 with interval +0.215 to +0.319 and validation R90 +0.329 with interval +0.255 to +0.402. This association was supported in broad, TP53, KRAS, PIK3CA, MYC, KEAP1, ARID1A, MTAP, mutation-burden, QC, batch A, co-mutation-restricted, and multi-negative strata, and validation of some strata such as H102 R179 reinforced the main association. The narrow H203 stratum and the KY-library PTEN-loss stratum H96 remained unresolved because intervals crossed the +0.10 threshold, but these small precision issues do not overturn the broad validated PTEN-loss and STAT3 dependency association. The most important negative and specificity findings are as informative as the accepted associations. Canonical oncogene-concordant dependency was generally not supported: KRAS mutation did not meaningfully predict dependency_KRAS, BRAF V600E did not predict dependency_BRAF, EGFR amplification did not predict dependency_EGFR, and ERBB2 amplification did not predict dependency_ERBB2. Cross-family RTK checks also rejected EGFR amplification as a predictor of dependency_ERBB2 and ERBB2 amplification as a predictor of dependency_EGFR. PIK3CA mutation did not meaningfully predict dependency_ERBB2 after excluding EGFR and ERBB2 amplification. MYC amplification was not clinically meaningful across most tested dependency outcomes, although one tiny opposite-sign WRN contrast appeared; it was far below +0.10 and was not promoted. MTAP loss did not have an independent meaningful association with dependency_USP7 or dependency_MDM2 outside TP53-loss context, and PTEN loss did not extend meaningfully to dependency_POLR2A, dependency_RPL11, dependency_KIF18A, dependency_DHX9, or TP53-wild-type dependency_MDM2. TP53 loss did not meaningfully predict dependency_MDM2 or dependency_STAT3, and KEAP1 loss did not meaningfully predict dependency_WRN or dependency_STAT3. MSI-high did not meaningfully predict dependency_DHX9, and research markers D, E, and F did not show central meaningful dependency effects, except that research marker F was rejected for dependency_STAT3 and dependency_USP7 and research marker E was rejected for dependency_SMARCA2. Many interaction hypotheses failed to clear the +0.10 benchmark or failed validation, including PTEN-loss by TP53-loss or KEAP1-loss modifications for dependency_STAT3, TP53-loss by KEAP1-loss or mutation-burden modifications for dependency_USP7, SMARCA4-loss interactions for dependency_SMARCA2, KEAP1-loss by SMARCA4-loss or mutation-burden interactions for dependency_NFE2L2, and MSI-high by SMARCA4-loss for dependency_WRN. These null interactions make the five main associations more credible as central within-dataset patterns rather than diffuse network effects. All conclusions are limited to the supplied dataset. They are associations among measured predictors and CRISPR dependency scores, not causal claims. External generalizability is not established, especially because the dataset is a standardized lung-cancer screen context with constructed assays and markers. Validation improves confidence that the central effects are not simple flukes within the primary split, but it does not establish mechanism or cross-dataset transportability. The most informative next analysis would be external replication in an independent lung-cancer dependency cohort with adequate counts for MSI-high, SMARCA4 loss, KEAP1 loss, TP53 loss, and PTEN loss. If only the supplied dataset can be used, the highest-value follow-up is a joint adjusted model evaluating all five central predictors simultaneously, with pre-specified interaction terms and multiplicity control, especially PTEN-loss by TP53-loss for dependency_STAT3 and TP53-loss by KEAP1-loss for dependency_USP7. The active registered negative controls H254 through H258 currently have no delivered evidence and do not change this final report; if analyzed, they can provide further outcome-specificity checks for MSI-high and another robustness check for PTEN-loss and dependency_STAT3 in ARID1A-loss cells.",
  "research_notes": "Final evidence state after iteration 22: no validation requests remain. H203 remains unresolved/closed using R208 and R216 because the narrow PTEN-loss STAT3 contrast is directionally supportive but imprecise and crosses the +0.10 benchmark in both discovery and validation. This does not weaken broad PTEN-loss STAT3 acceptance from R88/R90. Supported central within-dataset associations: MSI-high to stronger dependency_WRN, R6/R52; SMARCA4 loss to stronger dependency_SMARCA2, R8/R13; KEAP1 loss to weaker dependency_NFE2L2, H20 R20/R39; TP53 loss to weaker dependency_USP7, H15 R17/R26; PTEN loss to stronger dependency_STAT3, H93 R88/R90. Negative/nonspecific results: no canonical concordance for KRAS/BRAF/EGFR/ERBB2 dependencies; cross-RTK effects null; PIK3CA/ERBB2 null; MYC mostly null; MTAP independent effects null; PTEN does not extend to POLR2A/RPL11/KIF18A/DHX9; TP53 loss not MDM2 or STAT3; KEAP1 loss not WRN/STAT3; MSI-high not DHX9; research markers D/E/F null for central outcomes; most interactions null or failed validation. H96 and H203 remain unresolved but closed due smallness/precision. H254-H258 remain active without evidence as final discovery negative/robustness controls. Final claims are associations only, not causal and not established externally. Next analysis: external replication or joint adjusted model with pre-specified interactions and multiplicity control."
}
